thread:
maria: Hey team — running into a weird Vitest failure when mocking our JS SDK in edge-compatible tests. Tests pass in Node but fail in the edge/runtime config. Error: “SyntaxError: The requested module '/node_modules/@redwood/inference/dist/index.js' does not provide an export named 'createClient'”

kevin: Is the SDK published as CJS or ESM? If Node passes and edge fails, sounds like an ESM/CJS interop issue.

maria: SDK is ESM-first with named exports. We import like `import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference'`. In tests I do `vi.mock('@redwood/inference', () => ({ createClient: vi.fn(() => ({ request: vi.fn() })) }))` and vitest throws the export error during module graph resolution.

jordan: Vitest does dependency pre-bundling. Try forcing the package to be inlined. In vite config: 

```ts
// vite.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'
export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    deps: {
      inline: ['@redwood/inference']
    },
    environment: 'node'
  }
})
```

kevin: Also if your tests run in the browser-like environment or edge runtime, you may need `test.environment = 'happy-dom'` or keep node but ensure transformMode includes web deps. Another option: `test.deps.inline` + `test.transformMode = { web: [/node_modules/] }`.

olivia: If the SDK does conditional exports ("exports" map) that points to different ESM/CJS entrypoints, bundlers can pick the wrong one. Quick check: open `node_modules/@redwood/inference/package.json` and look at `exports`/`module`/`main`.

maria: package.json has "exports" with ./dist/index.js and dist contains both .mjs and .cjs. When vitest tries to pre-bundle it apparently resolves to the .cjs variant but then ESM import tries to re-export named exports and fails.

jordan: For a practical workaround in tests, you can mock via an async factory that returns an ES module shape. Example:

```js
// in test file
vi.mock('@redwood/inference', async () => {
  const actual = await vi.importActual('@redwood/inference')
  return {
    ...(actual as any),
    createClient: () => ({ request: vi.fn() })
  }
})
```

olivia: If that doesn't work, try switching mocking style to `vi.mock('@redwood/inference', () => ({ default: { createClient: vi.fn() } }))` — sometimes bundlers wrap exports under default for CJS.

kevin: Another angle: enable `esbuild` transform for node_modules in Vitest so named exports are preserved. In vite config: `esbuild: { target: 'es2022' }` and `test: { deps: { inline: ['@redwood/inference'], external: [] } }`.

maria: I tried `deps.inline` and the async factory. Still saw a different error: 

```
TypeError: createClient is not a function
    at Object.<anonymous> (src/__tests__/sdk.test.ts:12:15)
```

kevin: That usually means the mock shape differs from how it's imported. Try logging the imported module in the test before mocking: `console.log(await import('@redwood/inference'))` to see runtime shape.

maria: Did that. It logs `{ default: { createClient: [Function] } }`. So import under ESM loader yields default with nested exports.

olivia: So your code `import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference'` expects a named export, but the runtime gives `default.createClient`. That's the problem at bundle/test time. Fix options: update SDK publishing so exports expose proper named ESM fields, or adjust tests to import default and destructure during tests, or add an interop wrapper.

jordan: We should treat this as a DX bug — SDK should export consistent shape across environments. Filing an issue would help (publishing tooling + bundler test coverage). For now, quick patch in tests: 

```ts
// shim in setupTests.ts
const mod = await import('@redwood/inference')
if (mod.default && !mod.createClient) {
  // re-export named for tests
  Object.assign(mod, mod.default)
}
globalThis.__REDWOOD_INFERENCE_MODULE = mod
```
Then in tests import from that global or re-import the shimmed module. Ugly but works for CI.

maria: Ugh but fine for short term. One more question: Jest + ts-jest config differences — some folks recommended using babel-jest to transpile ESM. Does that apply here?

kevin: Yes, Jest historically struggles with pure ESM packages. ts-jest has ESM support but is finicky. If you use Jest, set `transform: { '^.+\.(t|j)sx?$': 'esbuild-jest' }` or `babel-jest` with `extensionsToTreatAsEsm: ['.ts']` and `transform: {}` config. But switching runtimes is heavy.

dev-bot: Reminder: there's an open SDK issue tracking ESM/CJS publishing inconsistencies — https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/issues/57 :eyes: 

maria: Thanks everyone. I pushed a temporary shim to `test/setupTests.ts` that copies default into named exports and added `deps.inline` for vitest. CI green locally. Filing a follow-up ticket to fix SDK packaging. :party: 

kevin: Good call. I'll add a checklist to the SDK onboarding docs to verify package.exports and include a small vitest snippet.

olivia: Also add a note in the JS SDK README about preferred bundler configs for edge runtimes.

maria: Closing this thread — saved the workaround in a PR (link: https://github.com/redwood-inference/webapp/pull/212).
